DTSTART;VALUE=DATE;VALUE=DATE:20260731
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Throughout your learning journey\, you have been working on dev
 eloping mission statements and exploring how your ideas can contribute to 
 transforming African communities. However\, a community is a puzzle\; ea
 ch mission is one piece of that puzzle. When combined\, your missions can 
 create a more complete and sustainable vision for change.\n\nThis assignme
 nt is designed to help you connect your individual missions\, collaborate 
 as a team\, and practise storytelling as a tool for impact.\n\n \n\nAssig
 nment Structure\n--------------------\n\n1. Intro Discussion (Mission Shar
 ing): The beginning of it all! \n----------------------------------------
 ------------------------\n\nEach member will share their mission statement
  with the group. \n\nThe goal of this stage is to listen\, understand\, a
 nd identify connections between your different missions.\n\nGuiding questi
 ons \n\n* What elements of your mission have you already started working 
 on?\n\n* What is the specific community you are engaging with?\n\n* What c
 hanges do you hope to achieve in that community?\n\nNote: Remember to reco
 rd your conversation in the [team task sheet.] (https://docs.google.com/sp
 readsheets/d/18VwVRsBEMcK0Pom9XZO_NPVsnZknHXHsfyDKVUTpOZ0/edit?usp=sharing
 ) Also\, prepare to work on your next discussion. Book office hours with y
 our learning coach\, and remember this will count towards your final grade
 !\n\n2. Follow-Up Conversation (Collaboration & Integration)\n------------
 -------------------------------------------\n\nThis part of the discussion
  should help you think about partnership\, interdependence\, and shared go
 als. Also\, remember that your goal is to ensure that there is growth in t
 he community. The fact that your mission is a success means that it contri
 butes to the success of another mission. Remember to consider that you are
  from different communities but still within Africa.\n\nGuiding questions:
 \n\n* What new lessons\, challenges\, or opportunities have you discovered
  in your mission that could be valuable for the whole group?\n\n* In what 
 ways can these lessons help strengthen or expand the impact of the communi
 ty you are serving?\n\n* If you were all working within the same community
 \, how could your missions complement one another?\n\nNote: Remember to re
 cord your conversation in the [team task sheet] (https://docs.google.com/s
 preadsheets/d/18VwVRsBEMcK0Pom9XZO_NPVsnZknHXHsfyDKVUTpOZ0/edit?usp=sharin
 g). Also\, prepare to work on your next discussion. Book office hours with
  your learning coach\, and remember this will count towards your final gra
 de! \n\n \n\n3. Closing Discussion (Storytelling & Pitch)\n-------------
 -------------------------------\n\nBreak the talk into acts or segments th
 at flow:\n\nAct 1 – Setting the Stage (Speaker 1): One student introduce
 s the shared challenge or problem in a story-driven way. They pose the cen
 tral question\, like\, what if our communities could be self-sufficient?\n
 \nAct 2 – Mission Stories (Speaker 2): The second person shares their pe
 rsonal mission and story\, tying it back to the bigger theme.\n\nAct 3 –
  Integration & Call-to-Action (Speaker 3): The final person shows how the 
 missions connect and closes with a powerful vision for the future. It's a 
 three-act talk\, but the challenge is for the students to deliver it as a 
 unified TEDx Talk by three storytellers and delivered as one story.\n\nNot
 e: Remember to record your conversation in the team task sheet. Also\, pre
 pare to work on your next discussion. Book office hours with your learning
  coach\, and remember this will count towards your final grade!\n\n \n\nD
 eliverables \n\n1. Submit an outline/plot map of your talk on Canvas\n\n2
 . A complete team task sheet on Canvas\n\n3. Prepare to deliver your talk 
 in a TEDx style! (Each group has 7 minutes to present). You are encouraged
  to use visual aids in your delivery. \n\nYour team will be graded on:\n\
 n* Storytelling Quality: How clearly and powerfully do you communicate you
 r shared vision?\n\n* Audience Engagement: Do you capture and hold attenti
 on through creativity\, voice\, and visuals?\n\n* Collaboration: Does your
  story reflect the integration of all group members’ missions?\n\n* Stor
 y delivery\n\n* Use VISUAL AIDS where necessary. \n\nRemember: This assig
 nment is not just about your individual mission. It’s about learning to 
 collaborate\, integrate\, and communicate in a way that mirrors how real c
 hange happens in African communities.
